1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,220
Every PLS-SEM model you run today

2
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:05,890
stands on more than a hundred years of ideas.

3
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:08,535
It's a story about intelligence tests,

4
00:00:08,535 --> 00:00:10,480
guinea pigs, economists,

5
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,220
and a teacher and his student

6
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:13,850
who took two different roads.

7
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,998
Let's go back to the beginning.

8
00:00:16,716 --> 00:00:18,136
1904.

9
00:00:18,136 --> 00:00:21,063
British psychologist Charles Spearman noticed

10
00:00:21,063 --> 00:00:23,876
that children who did well on one school subject

11
00:00:23,876 --> 00:00:25,666
tended to do well on the others.

12
00:00:25,716 --> 00:00:27,598
He argued that one hidden factor

13
00:00:27,598 --> 00:00:29,976
sat behind all of those scores.

14
00:00:29,976 --> 00:00:33,090
He called it g, for general intelligence.

15
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:35,280
That was the birth of factor analysis:

16
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,220
measuring something you cannot see

17
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:39,121
through the things you can.

18
00:00:39,909 --> 00:00:43,509
A few years later, American geneticist Sewall Wright

19
00:00:43,509 --> 00:00:45,797
was studying the coat patterns of guinea pigs.

20
00:00:45,847 --> 00:00:48,789
To separate heredity from environment,

21
00:00:48,789 --> 00:00:51,069
he drew arrows between variables

22
00:00:51,069 --> 00:00:53,439
and turned those arrows into equations.

23
00:00:53,489 --> 00:00:57,310
In 1921, he published Correlation and Causation.

24
00:00:57,310 --> 00:00:59,405
Path analysis was born.

25
00:01:00,125 --> 00:01:03,945
In the 1940s, economists like Trygve Haavelmo

26
00:01:03,945 --> 00:01:06,103
showed how whole systems of equations

27
00:01:06,103 --> 00:01:07,895
could be estimated together.

28
00:01:07,945 --> 00:01:12,513
And in the 1960s, sociologists such as Otis Dudley Duncan

29
00:01:12,513 --> 00:01:16,053
brought path analysis into the social sciences.

30
00:01:16,103 --> 00:01:18,043
Factors, paths, and equations.

31
00:01:18,043 --> 00:01:19,863
All the pieces were on the table.

32
00:01:19,863 --> 00:01:22,434
Someone just had to put them together.

33
00:01:23,172 --> 00:01:27,252
In the early 1970s, Swedish statistician Karl Jöreskog

34
00:01:27,252 --> 00:01:31,172
combined factor analysis and path analysis into one framework,

35
00:01:31,172 --> 00:01:33,902
and his program, LISREL, put it on computers.

36
00:01:33,952 --> 00:01:36,005
This is covariance-based SEM.

37
00:01:36,005 --> 00:01:37,912
It asks one big question:

38
00:01:37,912 --> 00:01:39,761
does my theory fit the data?

39
00:01:39,811 --> 00:01:43,251
But Jöreskog's own doctoral advisor, Herman Wold,

40
00:01:43,251 --> 00:01:44,301
took another road.

41
00:01:44,351 --> 00:01:46,291
Wold built a softer method

42
00:01:46,291 --> 00:01:49,391
that maximizes the variance explained in the outcomes.

43
00:01:49,391 --> 00:01:51,011
He called it soft modeling.

44
00:01:51,011 --> 00:01:53,256
Today we call it PLS-SEM.

45
00:01:53,982 --> 00:01:56,674
So there are two types of SEM.

46
00:01:56,724 --> 00:02:00,478
Covariance-based SEM is built to confirm theory.

47
00:02:00,478 --> 00:02:02,762
It treats constructs as common factors

48
00:02:02,762 --> 00:02:05,172
and judges the model by its overall fit.

49
00:02:05,222 --> 00:02:07,882
PLS-SEM is built to explain and predict.

50
00:02:07,882 --> 00:02:10,162
It treats constructs as composites

51
00:02:10,162 --> 00:02:13,967
and focuses on R squared and predictive power.

52
00:02:14,017 --> 00:02:15,357
Neither one is better.

53
00:02:15,357 --> 00:02:16,825
Choose by your research goal,

54
00:02:16,825 --> 00:02:18,873
not by your sample size.

55
00:02:19,642 --> 00:02:20,932
Then came the software.

56
00:02:20,982 --> 00:02:23,485
LISREL in the 1970s.

57
00:02:23,485 --> 00:02:25,722
Jan-Bernd Lohmöller's LVPLS,

58
00:02:25,722 --> 00:02:30,037
the first PLS path modeling program, in the 1980s.

59
00:02:30,037 --> 00:02:32,052
And EQS in 1985.

60
00:02:32,102 --> 00:02:36,562
In the 1990s, Amos brought drag-and-drop path diagrams,

61
00:02:36,562 --> 00:02:39,962
Wynne Chin's PLS-Graph did the same for PLS,

62
00:02:39,962 --> 00:02:43,158
and Mplus arrived in 1998.

63
00:02:43,208 --> 00:02:45,748
In 2005, SmartPLS put PLS-SEM

64
00:02:45,748 --> 00:02:49,198
within reach of almost every student.

65
00:02:49,248 --> 00:02:52,068
Then WarpPLS in 2009,

66
00:02:52,068 --> 00:02:54,688
the free R package lavaan in 2012,

67
00:02:54,688 --> 00:02:58,520
and SmartPLS 4 in 2022.

68
00:02:59,215 --> 00:03:02,655
So the next time you open SmartPLS, remember:

69
00:03:02,655 --> 00:03:05,075
you are standing on more than a century of work,

70
00:03:05,075 --> 00:03:07,885
from Spearman's g to the model on your screen.

71
00:03:07,935 --> 00:03:09,715
Subscribe to Not Your Lecture

72
00:03:09,715 --> 00:03:13,484
for more research stories that actually make sense.
